> Janis Papanagnou <Janis_Papanagnou@hotmail.com> wrote:
> > Interesting also that Vlad/Dracula is based on an existing historical
> > person (didn't knew that).
> > | Some characters are based on historical persons:
> > |   * Vlad the Impaler
> Well, for some, exceedingly tiny, value of "based on". Bram Stoker read
> the name, liked it, and adopted it for his book. Apart from the nickname
> and a taste for blood, Vlad Dracul and count Dracula have very little in
> common. Dracula is a monster, period, who literally drinks blood; Vlad
> was a harsh ruler in a harsh time and a harsh environment, who defended
> his country against overwhelming enemies through a reputation of not
> being afraid to spill the stuff.

I think you mean "... Vlad Dracula and count Dracula ...".
Vlad III Dracula, the Impaler, is the Vlad usually associated with the 
fictional monster character. Vlad II Dracul was Vlad III Dracula's 
father (the -a ending meaning "son of").
I agree that Stoker's basis on this character is extremely thin, and 
possibly only really the last name.

